.TH DHCPIG 1 "2026-08-01" "dhcpig 2.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dhcpig \- DHCP exhaustion / network-hardening validation tool (whitehat)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dhcpig
.I command
.I interface
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B dhcpig ifaces
.br
.B dhcpig report
.I path
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dhcpig
validates that a network is hardened against DHCP pool exhaustion, forced
lease release, and gratuitous-ARP disruption, and passively fingerprints
hosts on the segment. It exhausts a DHCP pool, releases and re-acquires
neighbours' leases by name, evicts hosts off addresses with forged RFC 5227
ARP conflicts, and reports whether the network defended itself.
.PP
It needs root (raw sockets) and Python 3.11 or newer. A local web UI is
available as
.BR dhcpig-web (1).
.PP
.B There is no authorization gate.
.I release
and
.I exhaust
disrupt live clients by design, and
.B \-\-scope
is optional \- with none given, a run targets the whole network attached to
.IR interface .
Only run this against networks you own or are explicitly authorized to
test.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI exhaust " interface"
Consume the DHCP pool. Non-destructive to the pool itself (it competes for
free addresses rather than seizing bound ones outright), but it releases
every ARP-discovered neighbour's lease first and forges ARP conflicts
against what it re-acquires \- see
.B "WHAT EACH COMMAND DOES"
below. This is the default, most invasive mode.
.TP
.BI scan " interface"
Passive ARP + DHCP fingerprinting. Read-only: sends nothing, runs until
stopped (Ctrl-C). CLI only \- not available from the web UI.
.TP
.BI active\-scan " interface"
Active discovery: ARP-sweeps the scope and sends one DHCPINFORM to
fingerprint the DHCP server(s). Non-destructive, but
.B requires \-\-scope
(or a resolvable interface network) so its sweep stays bounded.
.TP
.BI release " interface"
.B DESTRUCTIVE.
DHCPRELEASE every in-scope, ARP-discovered neighbour's lease, re-acquire
the freed addresses by name (DHCP option 50), then contest ownership of
what it took with forged ARP conflicts (RFC 5227 \(sc2.4). The pool itself
is left intact, so most hosts can immediately get a new address; the
verdict is whether any host lost its address and failed to get a new one.
.TP
.BI release\-previous " interface"
Recovery, not an attack. Replays this tool's on-disk lease journal and
hands back every address a prior
.I exhaust
or
.I release
run took on this interface/network/server. Sends nothing if a new client
can already obtain an address.
.TP
.BI restore " interface"
Release exactly the leases the
.B currently running
process instance acquired (in-memory only \- useless once that process has
exited). For recovery after the process is gone, use
.I release\-previous
instead.
.TP
.B ifaces
List interface names usable with the other commands.
.TP
.BI report " path"
Re-render a previously saved JSON report file and print a one-line
summary to stdout.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Common to exhaust, scan, active\-scan, release, release\-previous
.TP
.B \-\-ipv6
DHCPv6 instead of DHCPv4.
.B Not currently implemented
\- every packet builder in this version is v4-only; passing this flag
raises a configuration error immediately rather than running a broken v6
listen / v4 send mismatch.
.TP
.BI \-\-report " FILE"
Write a JSON session report to
.I FILE
when the run ends. (The written format is always JSON regardless of
.IR FILE 's
extension; the web UI's Report tab additionally offers CSV and HTML.)
.TP
.BI \-v ", " \-\-verbosity " N"
Output detail, 0\(en3 (default 2). 0 prints results only (findings and the
final summary); 1 is a compact one-character-per-packet stream; 2 is the
normal packet-level log; 3 adds internal debug lines.
.TP
.BI \-\-status\-interval " SEC"
Emit a periodic status line with running totals every
.I SEC
seconds (default 5; 0 disables it). Shown only at
.BR \-v3 .
.SS exhaust only
.TP
.BI \-\-request\-option " SPEC"
DHCP option numbers to request (parameter request list), e.g.
.RI \(dq 12,14\-19,23 \(dq.
Defaults to requesting options 0\(en79.
.TP
.BI \-\-client\-mac " MAC"
Use this MAC for a simulated client instead of a random one. Repeatable;
with more than one, the sender rotates through the list round-robin
instead of generating random MACs.
.TP
.B \-\-spoof\-eth\-src
Ethernet source address equals the per-client MAC, so each simulated
client looks like a distinct device at Layer 2 (this is what exercises
port security / DHCP snooping). Default; this flag exists for symmetry
with
.BR \-\-no\-spoof\-eth\-src .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-spoof\-eth\-src
Use the real NIC's MAC as the Ethernet source for every frame, changing
only the BOOTP
.I chaddr
per client. Needed on Wi-Fi, where an access point drops frames whose
source MAC doesn't match the associated station.
.TP
.BI \-\-scope " CIDR"
Bound the run to this network. Repeatable. Defaults to the interface's own
network. Supplying it also makes the pool-size estimate in the report
deterministic (a host count from the CIDR) instead of inferred from the
first DHCPOFFER's subnet.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Reconnaissance only: the ARP sweep and the legitimate before/after DHCP
control transactions run for real (they need a raw-capable interface and
root), but nothing that would take or disturb a lease is sent \- no
RELEASE, no re-acquisition flood, no ARP conflict.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-journal
Don't record acquired leases to the on-disk recovery journal (default:
record them). Without it,
.I release\-previous
has nothing to recover with if this run is interrupted.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-evict
Skip the ARP-conflict eviction phase against re-acquired addresses
(default: run it). See RFC 5227 \(sc2.4.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-race\-freed
Skip firing a targeted DISCOVER the moment a live foreign DHCPNAK or
DHCPDECLINE shows a specific address just became free at the server
(default: race it, ahead of the untargeted flood).
.TP
.B \-\-race\-on\-rediscover
Also race on a foreign DHCPDISCOVER from a MAC the ARP inventory already
has an address for. Off by default \- the highest-volume, lowest-precision
trigger of the three.
.SS active\-scan, release, release\-previous
.TP
.BI \-\-scope " CIDR"
Network(s) to sweep / target / recover. Repeatable. Defaults to the
interface's own network.
.I active\-scan
refuses to run if no scope can be determined at all.
.TP
.BI \-\-rate " N"
Send rate in packets per second (default 7; 50 on
.IR release\-previous ,
see below). Not available on
.I exhaust
\- its windowed handshake pipeline self-paces instead.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
See above.
.SS release only
.TP
.B \-\-no\-evict
Skip the ARP-conflict eviction phase (default: run it).
.I release
shares the same release / re-acquire / evict chain as
.IR exhaust ,
minus the pool-draining flood.
.SS release\-previous only
.TP
.BI \-\-journal " PATH"
Override the lease journal location (default: the resolved
per-interface path under
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/dhcpig/ ).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-age " DAYS"
Ignore journal entries older than this many days (default 7). An
optimisation, not a safety measure \- a stale entry's MAC simply won't
match the server's current binding.
.TP
.B \-\-any\-server
Release journal entries even when the currently reachable DHCP server
differs from the one recorded at acquisition time (default: skip
mismatches, to avoid releasing addresses on the wrong network).
.TP
.BI \-\-rate " N"
Default
.B 50
here, not 7 \- this mode runs during an outage the operator is actively
trying to end, and its frames are unicast to one server rather than
sprayed at the segment, so a faster default is the right trade-off.
.TP
.BI \-\-passes " N"
DHCPRELEASE has no reply (RFC 2131), so resend the selected set this many
times as cheap insurance against a dropped frame (default 2).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
Bad arguments / configuration error (e.g.
.I active\-scan
with no resolvable scope,
.BR \-\-ipv6 ).
.TP
.B 3
No DHCP server detected before the no-server deadline elapsed.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted (Ctrl-C).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/dhcpig/leases-<iface>.jsonl
(or
.I ~/.local/state/dhcpig/...
if
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset) \- the append-only lease journal that
.I release\-previous
replays. Per-engagement data; delete it when an engagement ends.
.SH WHAT EACH COMMAND DOES
See
.I README.md
in the source distribution for a walkthrough of each mode's packet
sequence, and
.I docs/DESIGN.md
for the reasoning behind the safety model (what still bounds a run now
that there is no authorization gate) and the evidence model (why a
verdict can be trusted).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Exhaust a pool and write a JSON report:
.B sudo dhcpig exhaust eth0 --report run.json
.TP
Release active clients in one /22, DESTRUCTIVE:
.B sudo dhcpig release eth0 --scope 192.168.4.0/22
.TP
Read-only discovery of DHCP servers on a scope:
.B sudo dhcpig active-scan eth0 --scope 192.168.4.0/22
.TP
Undo a previous run from its lease journal:
.B sudo dhcpig release-previous eth0
.TP
Recon only, nothing mutating sent:
.B sudo dhcpig exhaust eth0 --dry-run
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Only run this against networks you own or are explicitly authorized to
test.
.I release
and
.I exhaust
disrupt live clients on the segment by design, and, absent
.BR \-\-scope ,
target the entire network attached to the given interface. The authors
are not responsible for misuse.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dhcpig\-web (1),
.BR pig (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Kevin Amorin
